For each index, the answer depends on the tallest bar to the left and the tallest bar to the right. Two arrays store these values so that each water amount can be found immediately.

For example, with `[3, 0, 2, 0, 4]`, the bar at index 1 has a left limit of 3 and a right limit of 4. It holds `min(3, 4) - 0 = 3` units.

The empty array and arrays with fewer than three bars return zero before any array access.

```java
class Solution {
    public int trap(int[] height) {
        int n = height.length;
        if (n < 3) {
            return 0;
        }

        int[] leftMax = new int[n];
        int[] rightMax = new int[n];

        leftMax[0] = height[0];
        for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
            leftMax[i] = Math.max(leftMax[i - 1], height[i]);
        }

        rightMax[n - 1] = height[n - 1];
        for (int i = n - 2; i >= 0; i--) {
            rightMax[i] = Math.max(rightMax[i + 1], height[i]);
        }

        int totalWater = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            int waterLevel = Math.min(leftMax[i], rightMax[i]);
            totalWater += waterLevel - height[i];
        }

        return totalWater;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.

Space complexity: `O(n)` because of the two maximum arrays. The input array is not changed.

Two pointers start at the ends of the array. The smaller current wall decides which side can be handled safely. A running maximum on that side tells how much water sits above the current bar.

This avoids storing left and right maximum arrays. An increasing array, a decreasing array, or an array with only one or two bars naturally produces zero.

```javascript
class Solution {
    trap(height) {
        if (height.length < 3) {
            return 0;
        }

        let left = 0;
        let right = height.length - 1;
        let leftMax = 0;
        let rightMax = 0;
        let totalWater = 0;

        while (left < right) {
            if (height[left] <= height[right]) {
                leftMax = Math.max(leftMax, height[left]);
                totalWater += leftMax - height[left];
                left++;
            } else {
                rightMax = Math.max(rightMax, height[right]);
                totalWater += rightMax - height[right];
                right--;
            }
        }

        return totalWater;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.

Space complexity: `O(1)`. The input array is only read, not changed.

This keeps one array for maximum heights from the left. Then the array is scanned from right to left while `rightMax` is updated. At each position, the left value and the current right value are enough to calculate the trapped water.

For `[4, 2, 0, 3, 2, 5]`, the left maximum at index 2 is 4 and the right maximum is 5. The water there is `min(4, 5) - 0 = 4`. Adding all positions gives 9.

An empty input, a flat array, and an array with no valley all return zero. The code does not change `height`.

```go
func trap(height []int) int {
    n := len(height)
    if n < 3 {
        return 0
    }

    leftMax := make([]int, n)
    leftMax[0] = height[0]

    for i := 1; i < n; i++ {
        if height[i] > leftMax[i-1] {
            leftMax[i] = height[i]
        } else {
            leftMax[i] = leftMax[i-1]
        }
    }

    rightMax := 0
    totalWater := 0

    for i := n - 1; i >= 0; i-- {
        if height[i] > rightMax {
            rightMax = height[i]
        }

        waterLevel := leftMax[i]
        if rightMax < waterLevel {
            waterLevel = rightMax
        }

        totalWater += waterLevel - height[i]
    }

    return totalWater
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` with one left scan and one right scan.

Space complexity: `O(n)` for `leftMax`.

Kept the direct method here. For every bar, find the tallest bar on its left and right. The smaller one gives the water level above that bar.

This is easy to follow, but it scans the array many times.

```cpp
class Solution
{
public:
    int trap(vector<int> &height){
        int n = height.size();
        int totalWater = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            int leftMax = 0;
            int rightMax = 0;

            for (int left = 0; left <= i; left++) {
                leftMax = max(leftMax, height[left]);
            }

            for (int right = i; right < n; right++) {
                rightMax = max(rightMax, height[right]);
            }

            int waterLevel = min(leftMax, rightMax);
            totalWater += waterLevel - height[i];
        }

        return totalWater;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)`.

Space complexity: `O(1)`. The input array is not changed.

